{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong upward bias beyond ~7–10 day horizon: forecasts stay in the 71–74 range while realized prices mean-revert and then trend sharply lower. Apply a negative level shift that increases with horizon (small in week 1, large by weeks 3–4).",
    "Regime-change blindness: when the last 30d history contains a rollover from ~72–73 into a downtrend, the model extrapolates the prior high regime instead of switching. Add a downtrend detector (e.g., negative 5–10d momentum / lower highs) that forces the forecast path to follow the new bearish regime.",
    "Overshoot and persistence after spikes: after local highs (~72–73), the model predicts continued strength for weeks, but realized prices reverse quickly and continue falling. Introduce spike-reversal logic: after a spike, cap upside follow-through and increase mean-reversion speed.",
    "Underestimates downside tail risk: realized outcomes show a large drawdown into low/mid-60s within 2–4 weeks, while forecasts never approach that range. Add a volatility/convexity adjustment: widen forecast bands and shift the mean downward when recent volatility rises or momentum turns negative.",
    "Horizon-specific drift error: day 1–5 errors are modest, but error accumulates with time (forecast path too flat/high). Apply a horizon-dependent drift correction (progressively more negative slope) rather than a constant offset."
  ]
}
